---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Fig from "../../components/Fig.astro";
import "../../styles/service.css";
import "../../styles/case.css";

const SITE = "https://www.tmdesignengineering.com";
const url = `${SITE}/work/water-rocket`;
const jsonld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "@id": `${url}#article`,
    headline: "Water Rocket, a first-principles flight model validated to ±5% and used to optimise the design",
    description: "Case study: a first-principles model of a water rocket built from thermodynamics, compressible nozzle flow and flight dynamics, solved in MATLAB, validated to within 5% of experiment, and used to optimise the design with coupled structural constraints. Over 300 m with the heaviest payload.",
    url,
    author: { "@id": `${SITE}/#thomas-mckee` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE}/#business` },
    about: "Water rocket flight modelling and optimisation",
    isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Work", item: `${SITE}/#work` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Water Rocket", item: url },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Water Rocket Case Study, First-Principles Flight Model | Thomas McKee"
  description="Case study: a water rocket modelled from first principles, thermodynamics, compressible flow and flight dynamics, validated to within 5% of experiment and optimised in software with coupled structural constraints. Over 300 m with the heaviest payload."
  type="website"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
<main>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="svc-hero">
    <div class="wrap svc-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Case study · Engineering analysis</span>
        <h1>A water rocket, flown thousands of times <em>in software first.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">A first-principles model of a water-powered rocket, built from thermodynamics, compressible flow and flight dynamics, checked against real launches, then used to optimise the design. I was designer and performance lead, owning the mathematical model, its MATLAB implementation, the experimental validation, the optimisation, and the structural analysis of the pressure vessel.</p>
        <div class="svc-hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#contact">Start your project <span class="arw">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#process">See the process</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="spec" aria-label="Project summary">
        <div class="spec-head"><span>Project</span><span>CASE 04</span></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Role</div><div class="spec-v">Designer &amp; performance lead</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Domain</div><div class="spec-v">Thermofluids · flight dynamics</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Tools</div><div class="spec-v">MATLAB · Runge-Kutta (4,5)</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Built</div><div class="spec-v">1 launcher</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Validation</div><div class="spec-v">±5% vs experiment</div></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- RESULTS STRIP -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="headline">
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">&gt;300 m</div><div class="hl">with the heaviest payload the launcher could carry</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">±5%</div><div class="hl">of experiment across fill, pressure and firing angle</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">4</div><div class="hl">additions to the published models</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">1</div><div class="hl">launcher built, thousands of designs tested in simulation</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROCESS OVERVIEW -->
  <section class="band" id="process">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">The design process</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">How the project ran, end to end</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Building and flying hundreds of rockets would have been slow, hard to repeat and constantly thrown off by the wind. So I built the rocket in maths first and let the model do the searching. It's the same method I later used on the Volt 2 elements and firmware, against a production deadline.</p>
      <ol class="proc">
        <li><div><h3>Define the goal and the ground rules</h3><p>Carry the heaviest payload as far as possible, safely. Before writing a single equation I set out every assumption and the theory behind it, because a model is only worth having if its limits are clear.</p><div class="uses">Problem definition · stating assumptions</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Research the published models</h3><p>I built on the existing literature and looked for where it fell short, which is where my four additions came from.</p><div class="uses">Literature review · critical analysis</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Derive the model from first principles</h3><p>Newton's second law for a body losing mass, Bernoulli for the water jet, polytropic gas expansion, compressible nozzle flow and quadratic drag, in three derivations.</p><div class="uses">Thermodynamics · compressible flow · dynamics</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Implement and solve it</h3><p>I coded the model in MATLAB and solved it with a fourth and fifth order Runge-Kutta method.</p><div class="uses">MATLAB · numerical methods</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Validate it against real launches</h3><p>I tested the model against my own launches and against published experimental data.</p><div class="uses">Experimental testing · validation</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Couple the parameters and optimise</h3><p>Every design variable was linked to the others, so the search only ever tried designs that could actually be built.</p><div class="uses">Parametric modelling · optimisation</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Make it safe, then build it</h3><p>Hoop stress analysis set the allowable pressure with a safety factor, so the design met the relevant standards before it was ever pressurised.</p><div class="uses">Structural analysis · pressure vessel design · design to standards</div></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SKILLS -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Skills used</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">What this project called on</h2>
      <div class="skills">
        <div><div class="sk-h">Engineering</div><div class="sk-tags"><span>Mathematical modelling</span><span>Numerical methods</span><span>Parameter optimisation</span><span>Structural analysis</span><span>Pressure vessel design</span><span>Experimental validation</span><span>Design to safety standards</span></div></div>
        <div><div class="sk-h">Theory</div><div class="sk-tags"><span>Variable-mass dynamics</span><span>Bernoulli's equation</span><span>Polytropic expansion</span><span>Isentropic nozzle flow</span><span>Choked flow</span><span>Quadratic drag</span><span>Hoop stress</span></div></div>
        <div><div class="sk-h">Tools</div><div class="sk-tags"><span>MATLAB</span><span>Runge-Kutta (4,5), Dormand-Prince</span><span>Hand derivation</span><span>Launch testing</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 4.1 PROBLEM -->
  <section class="band" id="cs-4-1">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Case study 4.1 · Mathematical model</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Mathematically modelling the flight path from first principles, so thousands of parameters could be tested without thousands of launches</h2>

      <div class="stage">
        <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Problem</span></div>
        <div class="about-body">
          <p>The launcher had to deliver the heaviest payload it could, as far as it could, and stay safe. There are a lot of variables in play: vessel radius and length, wall thickness, water fill, pressure, nozzle size and firing angle, and they all affect each other. Testing them physically would have meant hundreds of builds and launches, each one affected by the wind on the day.</p>
          <p>A simulation was the answer, but only one I could trust. That meant deriving it properly, stating its assumptions openly, and proving it against real launches.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="callout"><b>Assumptions declared up front</b>: quasi-steady inviscid flow through the nozzle · water inertia and thrust concentration neglected, as the two largely cancel · polytropic expansion with γ from published data · constant ambient pressure, temperature and gravity · constant drag coefficient from wind-tunnel results · no wind, the largest divergence from reality.</div>
        <div class="figs">
          <Fig n="71" cap="Flight phases and the free body diagram, reproduced from sketch" />
          <Fig n="72" cap="Experiment against the literature models and my model" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 4.1 PROCESS -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase phase-solution">Process</span></div>
        <h3>Three derivations, a validation, and a coupled optimisation</h3>
        <ol class="steps-inline">
          <li><span class="sn">01</span><span><b>Derivation I, the water thrust phase.</b> Newton's second law on the vehicle, resolved along its flight path, with thrust, drag, weight and mass all changing over time. Bernoulli through the nozzle gives the jet velocity, and the thrust becomes a function of pressure alone. This is where I departed from the published model: the air expands <strong>polytropically</strong> rather than isothermally, and the vehicle mass includes the air still inside the bottle. The result is one first-order equation in pressure, which makes the whole water phase almost free to solve, and that's what makes sweeping thousands of designs practical.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">02</span><span><b>Derivation II, the air thrust phase.</b> Once the water has gone, the bottle is a fixed volume of compressed air. While the pressure is high the nozzle is <strong>choked</strong> at Mach one and pressure thrust has to be counted; below the critical ratio it unchokes. Most published models treat the whole air phase as unchoked, which throws away the pressure thrust exactly where thrust is highest, so splitting the two regimes was my third addition.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">03</span><span><b>Solve it accurately.</b> The literature uses a first-order method. I used a fourth and fifth order <strong>Runge-Kutta</strong> pair in <strong>MATLAB</strong>, which removes that source of error, my fourth addition.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">04</span><span><b>Validate against more than my own data.</b> I tested the model against my launches and against Barrio-Perotti et al.'s experimental results, since a model tuned only to its author's data proves very little. It held to within about 5% across water fill, pressure and firing angle, and predicted more closely than their model.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">05</span><span><b>Let the structure set the pressure.</b> The vessel is a thin-walled cylinder, so hoop stress governs: <span class="mono">P<sub>all</sub> = 2tσ<sub>y</sub> / (r·SF)</span>. Setting wall thickness as a fraction of radius, <span class="mono">t = βr</span>, collapses that to <span class="mono">P<sub>all</sub> = 2βσ<sub>y</sub> / SF</span>, which doesn't depend on radius at all. Radius is then free to be optimised for volume and drag, and the cost of a bigger vessel shows up in mass. A wall of 5 to 6% of the radius was the first point where the allowable pressure beat the cartridge pressure, so that became the design point.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">06</span><span><b>Couple the parameters so only legal designs are tried.</b> Changing the radius changes the wall thickness, which changes the mass, which changes the trajectory, and the allowable pressure caps what fill pressure can be tried. Written as functions in the code, an illegal combination simply can't be evaluated.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">07</span><span><b>Turn the firing angle into a function.</b> The best angle falls as pressure rises, and sweeping it inside every loop would have squared the run time. I ran angle against pressure once, fitted the ridge of the response, <span class="mono">θ<sub>opt</sub>(P) = θ<sub>∞</sub> + (θ<sub>0</sub> − θ<sub>∞</sub>)e<sup>−P/P*</sup></span>, and tested every design at its own best angle so the comparisons stayed fair.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="figs f3">
          <Fig n="73" cap="Range against pressure and firing angle" />
          <Fig n="74" cap="Radius against mass, allowable pressure and volume" />
          <Fig n="75" cap="Wall thickness study: where the vessel first survives the cartridge" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 4.1 RESULTS -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Results</span></div>
        <h3>Over 300 metres with the heaviest payload</h3>
        <div class="mini"><div><b>&gt;300 m</b>heaviest payload</div><div><b>±5%</b>of experiment</div><div><b>1</b>launcher built</div></div>
        <div class="about-body">
          <p>The launcher was optimised, made safe, and carried the heaviest payload it could over 300 metres. The structural analysis set the allowable pressure with a safety factor, so the design already met the relevant standards before it was ever pressurised.</p>
          <p>The method matters more than the number. A model validated against measurement let me test thousands of parameter combinations, where the alternative was building hundreds of prototypes and testing them one at a time. Establish the physics, validate it against reality, then let the model do the searching: it's the same approach I used on the Volt 2 five years later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figs">
          <Fig n="76" cap="Trajectory families for vessel radius at 4% wall thickness" />
          <Fig n="77" cap="Optimised design, firing over 200 metres on test" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW I HELP -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">How I could help you</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The same process, on your product</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">When physical testing is slow, expensive or unrepeatable, a model you can trust saves both time and money. Building and validating that kind of model is a big part of a <a href="/services/feasibility-study" style="color:var(--ember)">Feasibility Study</a> and <a href="/services/design-verification" style="color:var(--ember)">Design Verification</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="start">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta">
        <div class="inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">Let's talk</span>
          <h2 style="margin-top:14px">Too many variables to test by hand?</h2>
          <p>Book a free intro call and tell me about the problem. I'll tell you honestly whether a model would help and what it would take.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/#contact">Book a free intro call <span class="arw">→</span></a>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href="/#work">See more work</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
</Base>
